Pillar 2: Inhabitants
Persistent NPC 1: Jarl Idgrod Ravencroft
Sourced: living-world.md, Hjaalmarch section, verbatim — "a woman who has
visions, who is regarded as eccentric by the court and quietly respected
by her people." Her steward Aslfur "is more practically-minded and
handles most of what passes for administration."
Core Motivation: To protect her hold from something she can sense the
texture of but cannot name or prove. Sourced: "Her visions don't give her
specifics but they give her the texture of it — a wrongness that has
been building for months and cannot yet confront directly without
evidence."
Current State: Delivers the oblique "patient... now it is moving" line
(adventure2, already built) when the party arrives. She is talking about
the coven — she does not know about Ismara and will not resolve which
one she means.
Potential Evolution: living-world.md's stated outcome — "the vampire
coven can be resolved by the party or by Morthal's own slow process of
investigation eventually producing enough evidence for a response." If
resolved, "the townspeople gradually recover. Morthal's general
wrongness lifts." The frozen people from Ismara's nodes do not unfreeze
either way — the two threads never actually touch.

Persistent NPC 2: Alva
Sourced: living-world.md, Hjaalmarch section, verbatim — "a local woman
[who] is a vampire who has been feeding Hroggar false affection while
feeding on his family — she burned his house with his wife and daughter
inside to eliminate complications and has been using his grief as cover
for her continued presence."
Core Motivation: Continued, undetected feeding. Hroggar's grief is
useful cover, not a relationship she values on its own terms.
Current State: Embedded in town, not at the fort — this is the actual
thread that connects to Movarth's coven if the party investigates
Hroggar's burned house or asks who doesn't sleep at night (adventure2,
"The Marsh — the Coven," already built). UPDATED this session: Movarth
is dead and his coven fallen to Valerius Caelus (see adventure2's
Valerius addition); Alva was judged worth keeping and now answers to
his network instead of an independent coven. Her actual behavior toward
Hroggar is unchanged — nothing about the con on him is different.
Potential Evolution: If exposed or fought as an isolated local vampire,
treat as a standard vampire encounter — pull generic stats from GM
Guide/Reference tables. If the party ever connects her to Valerius
directly, that thread goes nowhere yet this adventure — it's a seed for
later, not a resolution owed this session.

Pillar 3: Events
Roll on this table for downtime in Morthal, or texture entering a new
part of town.
d10 | Event
1   | Someone Who Doesn't Remember: An NPC mentions, offhand, that they
    | "must have slept through" something everyone else in town is
    | talking about. They don't find this alarming. Nobody does yet.
2   | Idgrod's Visions: The Jarl is heard muttering something half-
    | prophetic to Aslfur, who nods like he's used to it. If the party
    | asks Aslfur to explain, he can't — "she sees what she sees."
3   | Hroggar, Grieving: Hroggar is seen at the site of his burned
    | house, or drinking alone at the Moorside Inn. He does not know
    | Alva is the reason. Play his grief as real, not suspicious.
4   | A Light in the Marsh: Something moves with purpose out past the
    | edge of town, toward the old fort, after dark. Nobody who lives
    | here goes looking. [This is Movarth's coven's activity, or
    | Ismara placing a node — GM's call which, or leave ambiguous.]
5   | The Frost Pattern: A standing figure is found on the marsh road,
    | further out than the Windhelm cases were. This one is Ismara's,
    | unambiguously (living-world.md's node-network geometry) — but
    | nobody in Morthal has the context to read it that way.
6   | A Missed Night: A townsperson can't account for where they were
    | the previous evening and brushes it off with visible unease
    | before changing the subject. A concrete instance of the "hollowed
    | out" texture, not just a description of it.
7   | Aslfur's Administration: The steward is handling some mundane
    | hold business — a grain dispute, a boundary complaint — with the
    | harried competence of a man doing two jobs because his Jarl's
    | attention is elsewhere. Texture, not plot.
8   | Quiet Day: The marsh is still. Order-of-business material.
9   | Idgrod's Direct Address: If the party has been in town more than
    | a day, Idgrod seeks them out herself rather than waiting for
    | them to return to Highmoon Hall — still oblique, still unwilling
    | to name a cause, but visibly aware they're the kind of people who
    | go looking at things.
10  | A Body, Drained: If the coven thread is live and unresolved,
    | someone turns up exsanguinated, blamed locally on "wild animals."
    | This is the concrete evidence Idgrod's own investigation would
    | eventually produce with or without the party (living-world.md's
    | stated ongoing-resolution path).
